WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.714
Welcome. This class answers a deceptively
simple question: what makes

2
00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:11.429
an AI system agentic? You will separate
model responses from

3
00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:17.143
fixed workflows and model-directed loops,
choose the simplest design that

4
00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:22.857
can satisfy a goal, bound state, tools,
budgets, exits, and

5
00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:28.571
human handoffs, and evaluate both actions
and outcomes. You will

6
00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:34.286
also stop and recover when a loop drifts,
repeats, exceeds

7
00:00:34.286 --> 00:00:40.000
authority, or loses a reliable path to
completion.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Begin by classifying the system, not its
brand. A single

9
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
model call turns an input into an output
without controlling

10
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
a continuing workflow. A deterministic
workflow uses code-defined steps and

11
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
branches, even if a model performs one
step. An agentic

12
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
loop gives a model some control over what
happens next:

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
it observes state, chooses a tool or
action, receives feedback,

14
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.000
updates state, and continues toward a
goal. A chat assistant,

15
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
coding copilot, or named agent may use any
of these

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
patterns in different interactions, so the
product label is not

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
enough. Ask who selects the next action,
whether tools can

18
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000
affect external state, what persists
between steps, and who decides

19
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
that the work is complete. Agentic
behavior is a property

20
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
of the run. It is not proof that the

21
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
system is autonomous in every context or
safe to grant

22
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
broad authority.

23
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.643
Suppose a team wants AI to reset customer
passwords. A

24
00:02:14.643 --> 00:02:19.286
single model response can explain the
approved reset process but

25
00:02:19.286 --> 00:02:23.929
cannot perform it. A deterministic
workflow can validate identity, issue

26
00:02:23.929 --> 00:02:28.571
one reset link, record the event, and
stop. An agent

27
00:02:28.571 --> 00:02:33.214
might inspect the account, choose among
recovery tools, request additional

28
00:02:33.214 --> 00:02:37.857
evidence, and adapt to exceptions. If the
accepted process has

29
00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:42.500
fixed identity and issuance steps, the
deterministic workflow is simpler

30
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.143
to verify and should remain in control.
Model assistance can

31
00:02:47.143 --> 00:02:51.786
help explain errors without choosing a
weaker identity check. Use

32
00:02:51.786 --> 00:02:56.429
an agent only if legitimate cases require
bounded adaptation that

33
00:02:56.429 --> 00:03:01.071
measured fixed flows cannot provide. Even
then, keep reset issuance

34
00:03:01.071 --> 00:03:05.714
behind deterministic identity, target,
rate, and audit controls. The useful

35
00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:10.357
design is not the one with the most
autonomy. It

36
00:03:10.357 --> 00:03:15.000
is the least complex system that reliably
meets the requirement.

37
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.071
When an agent is justified, define a run
contract before

38
00:03:21.071 --> 00:03:27.143
execution. State one bounded goal and
observable completion criteria. Identify

39
00:03:27.143 --> 00:03:33.214
initial state, permitted memory,
artifacts, and sources of truth. Give

40
00:03:33.214 --> 00:03:39.286
the agent the smallest tool set and scope
each credential

41
00:03:39.286 --> 00:03:45.357
to the task. Separate read tools from
consequential action tools.

42
00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:51.429
Define deterministic validation,
exact-target approval, and postcondition

43
00:03:51.429 --> 00:03:57.500
checks around every effect. Set maximum
turns, tool calls, elapsed

44
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:03.571
time, cost, retries, and concurrent
actions. Name success, ordinary failure,

45
00:04:03.571 --> 00:04:09.643
unsafe request, invalid state, repeated
non-progress, budget exhaustion, uncertain

46
00:04:09.643 --> 00:04:15.714
outcome, and human-handoff exits. At each
step expose the current

47
00:04:15.714 --> 00:04:21.786
goal, relevant state, selected tool,
resolved target, expected effect, and

48
00:04:21.786 --> 00:04:27.857
next stopping test. Start with one agent.
Add routing or

49
00:04:27.857 --> 00:04:33.929
specialist agents only when representative
evaluations show a simpler design

50
00:04:33.929 --> 00:04:40.000
cannot reliably handle distinct tasks.

51
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Choose one possible agent task. Write one
observable completion condition,

52
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:54.000
one repeated non-progress stop, one
maximum budget, one action requiring

53
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:01.000
exact human approval, and one handoff
condition.

54
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:37.429
Evaluate the trajectory as well as the
final answer. A

55
00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:43.857
plausible response can hide a wrong
target, excessive permissions, duplicate

56
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:50.286
action, unsupported claim, ignored denial,
or unnecessary twenty-step loop. Record

57
00:06:50.286 --> 00:06:56.714
the goal and run contract version, state
changes, tool choices,

58
00:06:56.714 --> 00:07:03.143
resolved arguments, approvals, results,
retries, handoffs, stop decision, and

59
00:07:03.143 --> 00:07:09.571
final postcondition evidence without
logging secrets or unnecessary personal

60
00:07:09.571 --> 00:07:16.000
data. Build representative cases for
ordinary success, missing information,

61
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.429
boundary values, permission denial,
malicious retrieved instructions, tool

62
00:07:22.429 --> 00:07:28.857
errors, timeout after an external write,
repeated non-progress, and recovery.

63
00:07:28.857 --> 00:07:35.286
Measure task completion, policy
compliance, tool and target accuracy,

64
00:07:35.286 --> 00:07:41.714
duplicate or forbidden effects,
unnecessary steps, latency, cost,

65
00:07:41.714 --> 00:07:48.143
escalation quality, and evidence coverage.
Check the real system state

66
00:07:48.143 --> 00:07:54.571
independently. The agent's summary is one
output to assess, not

67
00:07:54.571 --> 00:08:01.000
proof that the requested outcome occurred.

68
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.000
Checkpoint. An agent searches for a
required document, receives no

69
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:13.000
result, rewrites the same query, receives
no result, and proposes

70
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:19.000
another nearly identical search. It has
used eight of ten

71
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:25.000
allowed turns and has no new evidence.
Should the loop

72
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:31.000
continue, and what should the system do?

73
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.636
Stop the loop. The last searches produced
no new evidence,

74
00:09:34.636 --> 00:09:38.273
and another cosmetic rewrite has no
justified path to success.

75
00:09:38.273 --> 00:09:41.909
Preserve the queries, results, state, and
budget use. Report that

76
00:09:41.909 --> 00:09:45.545
the required document was not found. Hand
control to a

77
00:09:45.545 --> 00:09:49.182
person or use a materially different
source or method only

78
00:09:49.182 --> 00:09:52.818
if the run contract authorizes it. If you
chose to

79
00:09:52.818 --> 00:09:56.455
spend the remaining turns, revise the
criterion: a turn budget

80
00:09:56.455 --> 00:10:00.091
is an outer ceiling, not permission to
continue without progress.

81
00:10:00.091 --> 00:10:03.727
If you declared failure without preserving
the evidence gap, add

82
00:10:03.727 --> 00:10:07.364
the gap and attempted searches so recovery
does not repeat

83
00:10:07.364 --> 00:10:11.000
the same path.

84
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:16.333
Recover from verified state, not from the
agent's narrative. Stop

85
00:10:16.333 --> 00:10:21.667
on denied authority, invalid or stale
state, repeated non-progress, exceeded

86
00:10:21.667 --> 00:10:27.000
budgets, unsafe requests, failed checks,
or unknown consequential outcomes. Persist

87
00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.333
a checkpoint containing the run contract
version, safe state references,

88
00:10:32.333 --> 00:10:37.667
completed step identifiers, approvals,
tool results, postcondition evidence, open

89
00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:43.000
uncertainties, and next permitted
decisions. Reconcile the real system

90
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:48.333
before resuming. Classify each step as
verified complete, confirmed failed,

91
00:10:48.333 --> 00:10:53.667
unknown, or unattempted. Resume only after
the checkpoint still matches

92
00:10:53.667 --> 00:10:59.000
current policy, tools, data, and target
state. Compensation and rollback

93
00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:04.333
are new actions with their own authority
and failure modes.

94
00:11:04.333 --> 00:11:09.667
Disable a tool when it exceeds tested
behavior. Transfer control

95
00:11:09.667 --> 00:11:15.000
when the task needs judgment, credentials,
cost, or consequence outside

96
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.333
the run contract. A clean handoff says
what the goal

97
00:11:20.333 --> 00:11:25.667
was, what happened, what remains
uncertain, and what decision the

98
00:11:25.667 --> 00:11:31.000
person must make.

99
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:36.500
Imagine a support agent may approve
refunds below a policy

100
00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:42.000
threshold but finds conflicting order
records. It has read the

101
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:47.500
case, verified the customer identity, and
made no financial change.

102
00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:53.000
The correct handoff does not say, refund
failed. It states

103
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.500
the goal, identifies the two conflicting
records, lists the checks

104
00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:04.000
already completed, confirms that no refund
was issued, records the

105
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:09.500
remaining approval and reconciliation
decision, and provides the safe case

106
00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:15.000
reference. The person can resolve the
conflict without repeating identity

107
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.500
work or guessing whether money moved. A
handoff is a

108
00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:26.000
state contract, not a conversational
apology.

109
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.714
Place guardrails around the model because
the model cannot enforce

110
00:12:31.714 --> 00:12:37.429
its own authority. Authenticate the person
and workload. Scope credentials

111
00:12:37.429 --> 00:12:43.143
to the smallest tool and task. Use
allowlists and typed

112
00:12:43.143 --> 00:12:48.857
validation for tools, targets, arguments,
and outputs. Minimize data and

113
00:12:48.857 --> 00:12:54.571
treat retrieved pages, tool results,
memory, and messages from other

114
00:12:54.571 --> 00:13:00.286
agents as untrusted content. Apply exact
approval to destructive, external,

115
00:13:00.286 --> 00:13:06.000
costly, privacy-sensitive, and
permission-expanding actions. Enforce

116
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.714
call, turn, time, cost, and concurrency
limits. Sandbox code and

117
00:13:11.714 --> 00:13:17.429
file work. Record secret-safe audit
evidence and verify postconditions

118
00:13:17.429 --> 00:13:23.143
independently. Layer content, policy, and
deterministic business checks because no

119
00:13:23.143 --> 00:13:28.857
single prompt, classifier, model, or
reviewer covers every failure. Test

120
00:13:28.857 --> 00:13:34.571
target substitution, stale state, prompt
injection, permission denial, duplicate

121
00:13:34.571 --> 00:13:40.286
writes, limit exhaustion, and failed
handoffs. Fail closed when identity,

122
00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:46.000
target, permission, or outcome is
uncertain.

123
00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:51.625
Open the bounded agent loop activity.
Choose one adaptable task

124
00:13:51.625 --> 00:13:57.250
and compare a model call, deterministic
workflow, and agentic loop.

125
00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:02.875
Justify the simplest sufficient design.
Complete the run contract with

126
00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:08.500
goal, state, tools, authority, checks,
budgets, exits, handoff, and recovery.

127
00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:14.125
Trace one successful trajectory. Then
challenge it with repeated non-progress,

128
00:14:14.125 --> 00:14:19.750
malicious instructions inside tool output,
and a timeout after an

129
00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:25.375
external write. Save the postcondition,
audit, stop, and recovery evidence

130
00:14:25.375 --> 00:14:31.000
for every case.

131
00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

132
00:17:35.167 --> 00:17:39.333
classify agentic behavior, choose when a
deterministic workflow is sufficient,

133
00:17:39.333 --> 00:17:43.500
locate enforceable approval, stop a
non-progressing loop, and explain why

134
00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:47.667
trajectory evidence matters. Review the
class or return to the

135
00:17:47.667 --> 00:17:51.833
activity before submitting. The assessment
begins only when you choose

136
00:17:51.833 --> 00:17:56.000
Begin knowledge check.

137
00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Keep autonomy earned and bounded: choose
the simplest system, expose

138
00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:06.000
the loop, enforce authority, evaluate
every trajectory, and stop with

139
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:11.000
a recoverable handoff.
